---
/**
 * Affiliate disclosure — FTC 16 CFR Part 255 compliance.
 * Must be reachable in one click from every page (it is, via the footer).
 */
import PageLayout from '@layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { SITE, LEGAL, DISCLOSURE } from '@config/site';
---

<PageLayout
  title="Affiliate Disclosure"
  description={`How ${SITE.name} makes money, which affiliate programs we participate in, and the firewall between commercial relationships and editorial scores.`}
  effectiveDate={LEGAL.effectiveDate}
  lastUpdated={LEGAL.lastUpdated}
>
  <div
    class="not-prose mb-8 rounded-xl border-l-4 border-emerald-500 bg-emerald-50/70 p-5"
    role="note"
  >
    <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-emerald-800">
      FTC disclosure statement
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink-800">{DISCLOSURE.long}</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="how-we-make-money">1. How {SITE.name} makes money</h2>
  <p>
    {SITE.name} is free to read and carries no paywall. We fund the site two ways:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Affiliate commissions.</strong> If you sign up for a product through a tracked
      link on this site, the vendor may pay us a referral fee. You pay exactly the same price
      — commissions come out of the vendor's margin, never as a surcharge to you.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Display advertising.</strong> We serve ads through Google AdSense and comparable
      ad exchanges. Advertisers have no access to, or influence over, editorial content.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="what-this-means">2. What this means when you read a review</h2>
  <p>
    An affiliate relationship creates an obvious conflict of interest: we are paid more when
    we recommend products that convert. We manage that conflict with a hard process rule
    rather than a promise.
  </p>

  <h3 id="scoring-firewall">2.1 Scores are locked before monetisation</h3>
  <p>
    Testing is completed and scores are recorded before anyone checks whether an affiliate
    program exists for that product. The writer running the test does not know the commission
    rate. Affiliate links are added afterwards, by a different person, as a publishing step.
  </p>

  <h3 id="ranking">2.2 Ranking order is never sold</h3>
  <p>
    Position in any "best of" list reflects our tested scores only. We do not accept payment
    for placement, for inclusion in a comparison, or for a higher ranking. If a vendor asks,
    we decline and note the request in the article.
  </p>

  <h3 id="negative-coverage">2.3 We publish negative findings about partners</h3>
  <p>
    Several products we hold affiliate relationships with score below competitors we earn
    nothing from. Where a tool is the wrong choice for a given use case, we say so explicitly
    in a "skip it if" section — including when we would be paid for the signup.
  </p>

  <h2 id="programs">3. Affiliate programs we participate in</h2>
  <p>
    {SITE.name} participates in affiliate and referral programs operated by software vendors
    in the workflow automation, CRM and data enrichment categories, including but not limited
    to Zapier, Make, n8n, HubSpot, Airtable, Clay, Apollo.io and Pipedrive. We may join or
    leave programs at any time.
  </p>
  <p>
    We also participate in advertising programs where we are compensated for referring
    traffic. Commission structures vary — some pay a one-off bounty, others a percentage of
    first-year revenue. The structure is never disclosed to the testing team.
  </p>

  <h2 id="identifying-links">4. How to identify an affiliate link</h2>
  <p>Every monetised link on this site is marked in three ways:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A visible disclosure appears next to or directly beneath the call-to-action button
    </li>
    <li>
      The link carries <code>rel="sponsored nofollow"</code> attributes, which is Google's
      required markup for paid links and is machine-verifiable in the page source
    </li>
    <li>
      A disclosure banner appears near the top of every article that contains tracked links
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Links to vendor documentation, pricing pages cited as evidence, and third-party research
    are <strong>not</strong> affiliate links and carry no tracking.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cookies">5. Affiliate cookies</h2>
  <p>
    Clicking an affiliate link may place a cookie from the destination vendor on your device
    so they can attribute a later signup to us. Cookie duration is set by the vendor and
    typically ranges from 30 to 90 days. We receive aggregate conversion reports — counts and
    commission totals — not your personal information. See our{' '}
    <a href="/privacy-policy/">Privacy Policy</a> and <a href="/cookie-policy/">Cookie Policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="your-choice">6. You can avoid affiliate links entirely</h2>
  <p>
    If you would rather not use them, navigate to the vendor directly by typing their domain
    into your browser. You will get the same product at the same price. We would rather you
    trust the analysis than click the button.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ftc-compliance">7. Regulatory compliance</h2>
  <p>
    This disclosure is made in accordance with the United States Federal Trade Commission's{' '}
    <a
      href="https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/ftcs-endorsement-guides-what-people-are-asking"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
      target="_blank"
    >
      16 CFR Part 255: Guides Concerning the Use of Endorsements and Testimonials in
      Advertising
    </a>
    . We also comply with the UK Competition and Markets Authority guidance on clearly
    labelling paid promotion.
  </p>

  <h2 id="questions">8. Questions</h2>
  <p>
    If anything here is unclear, or you believe a link is not properly disclosed, email{' '}
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email.editorial}`}>{SITE.email.editorial}</a> and we will correct
    it. Partnership enquiries go to{' '}
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email.partnerships}`}>{SITE.email.partnerships}</a> — note that
    buying a link or a ranking is not something we offer.
  </p>
</PageLayout>
